{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate","id":1528172,"title":"V5 Discipline debate","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-01-07T14:52:38-08:00","id":1528172,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon1.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> V5 Discipline debate","content":"I'm primarily a lurker on these boards, so I didn't necessarily expect to start any threads on them ever. I definitely didn't expect my first thread to be one dedicated to porting a discussion from a different thread into its own space, but under the circumstances separating them seemed worthwhile. In all likelihood, there are a fair number of people involved in the Ministry Discipline thread who don't particularly care about the back-and-forth between Theodrim and myself within it, and the more we clog that thread up with it, the more of a nuisance we're likely to be to them.<br /> <br /> This conversation started over in <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527926-ministry-s-discipline?p=1527971#post1527971\">another thread</a> about preferences on the Setites/The Ministry across editions, or more specifically about Serpentis in previous editions vs their use of Protean in V5. Increasingly, related but distinct features of V5 Disciplines in general became the focus of this conversation, along with connected subjects such as how to effectively 'translate' prior edition power sets into the new edition, the part Disciplines play both thematically and mechanically in establishing clan roles and enabling or constraining the expression of character/clan identities, and whether V5 or preceding editions handle Discipline-related matters 'better'. At this point, I think the Serpentis vs Protean comparisons that were the start of this discussion are almost incidental to where it is now, and this strikes me as another good reason to move it.<br /> <br /> For the benefit of anyone who is interested in these subjects and/or the previous discussion but isn't entirely clear on where this discussion currently stands/is about in more concrete terms, and I suppose to organize my own thoughts a bit, I'll take a moment to categorize and summarize its various topics in the following three posts; I'm not sure that splitting them up this way actually helps, but the single post preview with all of them looked ridiculous to me. I'll start with some background information that may give perspective and then mark the starting point for the recap of the arguments themselves with double asterisks for people who are familiar with this information already. Feel free to pipe in if you think I've missed anything important/interesting and/or misrepresented anything in my overviews.<br /> <br /> (Edit: Whee, I got part I spam flagged. I'll hope it gets approved eventually but for now, I'll repost it out of order.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27894,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","user":"Paramecius","date":". 01-07-2024, 06:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","name":"Paramecius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27894_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-07T14:59:56-08:00","id":1528174,"title":"","content":"<b>I. V5 Disciplines: Progressions and costs</b><br /> <br /> The way that V5 approaches Disciplines is one of the biggest changes from the previous editions. V5 collapses the 30 plus Disciplines that the older editions features into a core set of 11 Disciplines that, aside from one exception that I can think of, always have multiple Discipline power options available for each level: with your first dot in Protean, for example, you have the choice between Eyes of the Beast, a power that lets you see in both mundane and supernatural darkness while also granting a bonus on Intimidation rolls against mortals, and Weight of the Feather, which protects from damage caused by falls and collisions while also potentially allowing you to avoid activating pressure-based devices like alarms or mines if the Storyteller decides that it should. You'll also find amalgam Disciplines that require dots in multiple Disciplines as part of the general power sets, and these are often (but not always) the form that this edition's versions of the near-exclusive clan Discipline powers from older editions take.<br /> <br /> For a good example of how V5 translates older signature powers into both amalgams and normal Discipline powers, let's take a closer look at Dementation. In order, its five starting powers in older editions are: Passion, The Haunting, Eyes of Chaos, Voice of Madness, and Total Insanity. Eyes of Chaos doesn't really exist in a singular form anymore: seeing the true Nature (as in Nature and Demeanor from prior editions) of others is covered by Scry the Soul from Auspex, foreshadowing future events is covered by Premonition from Auspex, and tracking patterns/impressions from objects is covered by The Spirit's Touch from Auspex. In V5, Dominate features an amalgam power requiring Obfuscate \u2022\u2022: Dementation. It is described as basically talking a victim into a nervous breakdown or psychotic break, the specifics of which are based on the victim's personality and emotional state/current Resonance, but tend to take the mechanical form of Willpower damage and behavioral Compulsions, which in V5 basically translate to &quot;do this or take a (usually -2) penalty&quot;. As you may already be aware, hallucinations/delusions and disturbed/confused thoughts and emotions are noted symptoms of psychotic breaks, so conceptually I'd say that the Dementation amalgam covers the same ground as Passion, The Haunting, and Voice of Madness; how well it works as a <i>functional</i> equivalent is a topic better suited for section III (though I won't get into it there either).<br /> <br /> But let's circle back to more general characteristics of Disciplines: your choice in Discipline powers is limited to one power per dot you gain in that Discipline, and your options include lower-level powers that you previously passed on for whatever reason. For example, at Presence \u2022\u2022\u2022, you could elect to gain a second level one Presence power or a level two Presence power instead of a level three one. The experience cost for Discipline dots in V5 increases the more you have just like in older editions: with V5, the only difference is that the cost is slightly higher because it multiplies the new level that you gain instead of the level at the time of the purchase. Regardless of their native levels, Discipline powers often have scaling characteristics: examples of this include the bonuses rising with the number of dots that you have in the Discipline and dice pools for Discipline powers increase by virtue of the fact that they frequently take the form of Attribute + Discipline. Since these scaling effects are based on the number of dots that you have in a Discipline, they are the same for any given power no matter what level you choose to purchase it at provided that as the Discipline's rating remains constant: getting Daunt at level one, for example, would still lead to it providing three bonus dice to Intimidation rolls at Presence \u2022\u2022\u2022 in the same way that getting it as your power of choice for Presence \u2022\u2022\u2022 would. Naturally, Daunt would have cost you 5-7 experience points at level one, as opposed to 15, 18, or 21 experience points at level three.<br /> <br /> They're rare, but there are also times when you may actually have to purchase a lower level power as you progress in a Discipline since higher level powers may have lower level powers that you didn't choose as prerequisites (or amalgam requirements like I mentioned above); the two Protean \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 options, Horrid Form and Metamorphosis, are examples of this.<br /> <br /> **For my part, regardless of whether you want to purchase a lower level power at a higher level or you <i>have to</i>, the fact that doing so can cost you twice as it would have earlier on, if not more, does not strike me as a good design choice.<br /> <br /> There are two reasons that Theodrim provides for why this isn't actually an issue that I want to discuss here: 1) the way that Disciplines scale means you're never 'just' getting a lower level power when you choose it at higher levels, and 2) some Disciplines are designed in a way where the native choices for certain levels can more comfortably be passed over.<br /> <br /> I do agree that scaling effects described here could reasonably factor into the value of any dot you purchase in a Discipline, but I don't agree that this tendency for various Discipline effects to scale is enough to balance things out to the point that cost disparities involved in getting a lower level power earlier as opposed to later don't matter. I have three main reasons for this: 1) a significant component of scaling benefits from Disciplines will apply regardless of what powers you choose, 2) it is often the case that scaled effects of lower level powers still won't match up to the impacts of higher level powers, and 3) there are, in fact, an assortment of powers that don't actually scale in any way.<br /> <br /> As far as reason #1 goes, the component I'm referring to is dice pool increases that come from adding dots to a given Discipline: taking Daunt for Presence \u2022\u2022\u2022 definitely does improve your dice pools for all Presence powers that roll Presence in them, but it's also true that taking Dread Gaze, a level three Presence power, provides this same benefit. This may well be the most important scaling effect of all for many builds since it can boost multiple powers, and it still applies even if you never choose a lower level power over a higher level.<br /> <br /> The respective effects of the powers themselves are still a significant point of difference, though, and the Daunt vs Dread Gaze provides a useful framework for discussing reason #2. At Presence \u2022\u2022\u2022, Daunt adds three bonus dice to Intimidation roll, like I mentioned above, while Dread Gaze can prevent mortals from acting even on a failure, send mortals running and keep vampires from acting during a turn (where the baseline assumption for combat in this edition is that it shouldn't last more three turns per character) with a success, or render mortals catatonic with fear/make vampires succumb to terror frenzies. Theodrim is right that Dread Gaze comes with costs and drawbacks of its own. It requires a Rouse Check to use, potentially leaving you hungrier and less in control if you fail, it can be a Masquerade risk, and hell, I throw in one more: it could end up scaring people way more than you actually want to. Even so, I'd also consider what a power uniquely brings to the table as an important factor in gauging its potential impact: with Dread Gaze on the table, making people lose their **** from fear is introduced as a baseline expectation when it makes sense to use it, and this is not in any way guaranteed through the successful use of Intimidation alone. Compare this to Daunt as a choice for Presence \u2022\u2022\u2022: the meat of it here is the bonus to Intimidation, which can be replicated more cheaply in a variety of ways. Free substitute options include leveraging teamwork bonuses, spending Willpower for rerolls, and/or making Rouse Checks for bonuses, while non-free but less expensive options include buying dots in Intimidation itself, buying Specialties for the Skill, buying Merits or Loresheet Advantages that can also improve Intimidation, and purchasing lower level powers for other Disciplines that also boost Intimidation, such as Eyes of the Beast or Shadow Cloak, while you can still get them on the cheap. I don't intend to suggest that Daunt is <i>useless</i> by any means - I actually think it's pretty good for what it is (which is <i>not</i> a level 3 Discipline power) - or that these alternatives are perfect replacements for Daunt's benefits, but I am saying that they can cover enough ground that it's hard to justify Daunt as an equally sound investment for Presence \u2022\u2022\u2022 if you want to scare people. I would be remiss to not acknowledge that Daunt stacks with these alternatives as well, but I don't think this significantly changes the equation. Experience points are a particularly precious resource in V5 given how slowly they will accrue by default (1 per session), so it will typically make more sense to settle for being merely 'good' at Intimidation to keep room for other options rather than sacrifice these options so that you can be 'absolutely amazing' at Intimidation.<br /> <br /> But let's say whoever is reading this is still not convinced that I've made an effective case for Dread Gaze over Daunt. Fair enough; maybe I'm wrong with this example. There are plenty of other power choices to consider: is getting Draught of Might, which lets you temporarily bestow two dots of Potence on someone else, for Potence \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 as good as getting Fist of Caine, which lets you deal aggravated damage with Brawl/Melee attacks for a full scene in an edition where that pretty much isn't a thing otherwise? Or how about getting Weight of the Feather instead of Shape Change or Metamorphosis, which along with its general utility may also let you avoid taking damage from falls by <i>flying</i>, depending on your choice of animal form? Would Cat's Grace, which lets you auto-succeed on rolls to keep your balance, make sense to purchase over Fleetness, which adds Celerity to Dexterity rolls out of combat and occasionally to Dexterity rolls for defense, when their cost is equal? So long as there is a reasonable basis for expecting more impactful effects/options from higher level powers as a general rule, then Theodrim's second point about the power options at certain levels for certain Disciplines making the rising costs for lower level powers easier to swallow just introduces a different problem: it doesn't raise the actual value of lower level powers, it just drags particular higher level powers closer down to their level.<br /> <br /> Still, a given reader's answer might be 'yes' to all of the questions I asked above and to any other lower vs higher level power comparison questions that I could come up with besides. Even then, scaling can only reasonably be counted as a mitigating factor for increasing experience costs for these lower level Discipline powers when one's chosen Discipline powers <i>scale at all</i>, which brings us to reason #3. As far as non-scaling Discipline powers go, this includes lots of Celerity powers and every Protean power from the core book, along with various others I listed in the other thread. Any calculations that assume scaling will pick the slack will go out the window if most or all of one's set of Discipline powers don't scale, and even one or two would throw off the balance as the level differences, and thus experience cost differences, grow more pronounced. In response to this, Theodrim asked me if non-scaling powers need to scale in order for them to be good: they don't necessarily (even if I think that a lack of any scaling benefits whatsoever will probably further limit their ability to punch above their weight class), but that does nothing to change the simple fact that <i>scaling has to occur</i> for it to balance out high vs low level Discipline power costs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27894,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","user":"Paramecius","date":". 01-09-2024, 05:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","name":"Paramecius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27894_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-07T15:04:41-08:00","id":1528175,"title":"","content":"<b>II. V5 Disciplines, Identity, and Interactions</b><br /> <br /> Like I mentioned earlier, the number of Disciplines in V5 has been drastically cut down, both to eliminate redundancies and to put some more distance between more niche clans and their representative Disciplines/powers (e.g., the Lasombra and Obtenebration, the Ravnos and Chimerstry, the Tzimisce and Vicissitude, etc.) to open up space for them to develop and express their themes in other ways. Even with Disciplines that are shared by many clans, an expanded range of individual options per level can still produce meaningful differences in how these Disciplines, and the clans that share them, function both through the individual powers themselves and their potential to interact with other powers in interesting/novel ways. Naturally, this also applies to potential variations between individual Kindred of the same or different clans.<br /> <br /> **I think that's basically all the background needed for this section, and there's less to pick apart and sort through on my disagreements with Theodrim here. Our difference of opinion mainly concerns the part that clan amalgams, which I'll use as a shorthand for the sorts of amalgam-based ports of signature powers from prior editions that I described in the background info for part I, play in all this. We both agree that exclusive (or near-exclusive) access to specific Disciplines and powers runs counter to fundamental design goals for this edition, but for my part, I argue that clan affinities and blind spots in the form of having an easier or more difficult time of accessing particular powers, especially clan amalgams, remains an important port in exploring clan identities. If there's a particular power, or set of powers, that is supposed to reflect a special niche for a given clan that distinguishes it from others, then making these clans work harder to attain the power(s) helps reinforce the identities of both the clan that claims this niche and the ones that don't. I don't think that any one power or set of powers is the be-all and end-all for clan identity, but that doesn't mean that powers, and what the ease or difficulty with which the various clans attain such powers says about them lore-wise or identity-wise in turn, should be dismissed as unimportant.<br /> <br /> It can be worthwhile to discuss whether a given clan can or should claim a particular niche as their thematic 'turf', and maybe it's not so much that Theodrim doesn't think this easy vs. hard access distinction for powers isn't worth maintaining so much as certain niches shouldn't be the privileged territory of one clan because of thematic overlap with other clans: the example we discussed is whether opening up the Ravnos clan amalgams of Chimerstry and Fata Morgana to Setites/The Ministry is a good or bad thing on balance, and the matter of thematic overlap is of particular significance here. I agree that it's interesting and fitting for Ministers/Setites to add illusions to their arsenal as corruptors, but what it takes away from the Ravnos tempers my enthusiasm because there's no real balance or reciprocity in the exchange.<br /> <br /> At least according to me. Theodrim has also argued that what access to Obfuscate and Presence opens up to them more than makes up for any drawbacks from adding Chimerstry to the Setite/Ministry wheelhouse. As far as clan identity goes, I don't quite agree: it's an expansion of common options at the cost of unique advantages, and I consider that a wash here. It's also arguable whether there is much of an addition here at all. They were always going have three Disciplines and Chimerstry was always going to function in part through Obfuscate in this edition: tying it to Presence rather than Fortitude does less for the clan's trickster/daredevil schtick when you consider that Fortitude also has options to resist mind control and conceal motives in this edition. Obfuscate + Fortitude for Chimerstry and Fata Morgana has the advantage of allowing for expanded general options while retaining advantaged clan access to both, but if I absolutely had to choose between them, I would probably be more inclined to find in favor of the expansion of common options if there were more of these options. Alas, there aren't: even at this stage in V5's development it's not uncommon to find two Discipline powers to a given level, and even the exceptions come with the proviso that they're often clan amalgams themselves with the added costs that this usually entails. This isn't me disagreeing with earlier me that higher clan amalgam costs are a good thing for clan identity; it's just me saying that these costs have to be considered when evaluating how many opportunities are really opened up here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","name":"Paramecius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27894_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-07T15:13:36-08:00","id":1528177,"title":"","content":"<b>III. V5 Disciplines vs Legacy Disciplines</b><br /> <br /> The argument <i>is</i> the background for this section, so no asterisks here.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure how much of this discussion is really conducive to constructive discussion given the ease with which cross-edition comparisons like this can devolve into edition warring, but in the interests of discouraging the latter I'll focus less on the bits about how much the Ventrue and Setites/Ministry's old Discipline constraints purportedly made them suck at living up to their archetypes and lore in past editions and more on the general contention that V5 provides more options in terms of Discipline powers and more opportunities to establish or introduce twists on player/clan identities in more novel and varied ways as a result. Having more options for Discipline powers per level might make this seem self-evident, but it's not actually as clear-cut as that. Theodrim admits that there's a Merit in past editions to add to one's existing clan Discipline spread, much like certain Loresheets allow you to in V5, and the requirements for learning out-of-clan Disciplines in V20 are no more onerous than in V5. It's probably quite a bit less onerous to invest in out-of-clan Disciplines in earlier editions since Disciplines themselves cost less per dot and the default arrangement was more experience per session and per story.<br /> <br /> Of course, this does nothing to address the argument that V5 offers more options per Discipline, but there are two key counters to this: the way Disciplines open up at six dots and above, and the existence of combination Disciplines. I mention 6+ dot Discipline powers more to acknowledge that they exist and contradict the claim rather than to suggest that I expect players normally get them; combination Disciplines are more important here they are available in addition to rather in place of the standard Discipline powers, in marked contrast to how V5 approaches Discipline amalgams. There's also the fact that some things that are now Discipline powers in V5 used to be Merits in prior editions: Cat's Grace being a 1-point Merit called Catlike Balance and much of the scope of V5 the Vicissitude amalgam being covered by the Vicissitude Modifications Merit in earlier editions are examples that come to mind. Also, it is noteworthy some V5 powers themselves are separated components of what individual powers in previous editions could do, like the earlier example I provided of what the functional components of Eyes of Chaos map to in V5 (I don't intend this as a particular criticism of V5 since I tend to agree that Dementation itself is largely of a muddle of tricks from other Disciplines that require less ST fiat to make work in play); similarly, Shape of the Beast has been broken up into Shapechange and Metamorphosis for Protean \u2022\u2022\u2022 and \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 respectively in V5. On top of that, there's the fact that certain older 1-5 dots Discipline powers no longer exist at all in V5, such as Astral/Psychic Projection, pretty much anything from Valeren aside from Corpore Sano, and various Thaumaturgy/Necromancy Path powers; the extent to which their absence is a good or bad thing will vary according to taste, but they are quite clearly options that no longer exist in V5.<br /> <br /> I'm not entirely sure where I land on the issue of which Discipline setup provides more opportunities for unique characterization, but it's definitely not a settled manner in favor of V5.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","name":"Paramecius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27894_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-13T03:39:00-08:00","id":1528477,"title":"","content":"I've always found the demand for no unique disciplines pretty weak. Not In the least if you're playing v5 corebook 3 groups have unique disciplines anyway.<br /> <br /> It ultimately seems to be more an appeal to a sense of 'neatness' and dislikes of specific ones with rationales coming from that. The biggest advocate against them unironically told me without necromancy the giovanni: a clan of mercantile degenerates centered around a human family which is university despised by other clans for their dark secrets is boring. Then turned around and defended clan tremere aka clan wizard with the wizard discipline whose wizarding ways dominates every aspect of the clan and discipline is super unique and awesome in every edition .","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 01-13-2024, 07:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-13T10:01:13-08:00","id":1528487,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar26986_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"26986\" data-vbnamecard=\"26986\">Ragged Robin</a> I was going to ask about the third when I remembered you said 'groups', not clans, and Thin-blood Alchemy popped into my head. Good catch.<br /> <br /> It's probably verging on a cliche at this point, but I prefer the way Requiem 2e approaches Disciplines. That is, the majority of the time particular bloodline or other powers will still be expressed as Devotions (which are similar to combination Disciplines and Discipline amalgams but can also include unusual powers from a single Discipline) with ties to one or more of the ten 'standard' Disciplines, or powers/rituals tied to Covenant Disciplines like Cr\u00faac or Theban Sorcery, but they aren't so committed to this setup that there's no room whatsoever for new Disciplines to be introduced when they make sense.<br /> <br /> I consider the awkward contortions in V5 Oblivion's setup to be decently representative of the complications that can arise when you get too attached to the idea of eliminating all unique Disciplines: from the start, the Ceremony power requirements are just kind of shoehorned in there (like using the corpse disposal power to reanimate corpses, the Shroud-sensing power to make mini zombie spies, or the flesh/food-rotting power to let a wraith inhabit your body) and it's obviously not future-proofed for any new Oblivion powers on the Necromancy side of its wheelhouse that crop up later.<br /> <br /> I also find it ironic that developing Oblivion simply as the necromantic counterpart to Blood Sorcery wouldn't have even run afoul of this 'no unique Disciplines' imperative if they hadn't insisted on collapsing every necromancy-using bloodline into clan Hecata as well, but I suppose that would still leave the question of what to do with Obtenebration for V5 (Obfuscate + Potence amalgams give us Nosferatu who can shadow-bend with the best of them (which isn't <i>not</i> cool to me, but again, mixed feelings), and Dominate + Obfuscate + Potence for more fine/sustained control over manifested darkness might get a bit cumbersome).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","name":"Paramecius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27894_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-13T21:47:11-08:00","id":1528524,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1528477#post1528477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've always found the demand for no unique disciplines pretty weak. Not In the least if you're playing v5 corebook 3 groups have unique disciplines anyway.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's true in every edition, in which the core rulebook only includes the original seven Camarilla clans. And in this case, it's not even factually correct given Caitiff are in the core rulebook and are explicitly by RAW capable of starting with any discipline. Even taking Caitiff out of the question as I'm sure for some reason they &quot;won't count&quot;, Protean isn't even unique as two of the ten predator types in the book give a free dot of Protean...and one of those two is the predator type that gives you a free dot of blood potency to boot.<br /> <br /> That brings the number of &quot;unique disciplines anyway&quot; down to...one. I hesitate to even include TBA on a list of disciplines, as it functions far more like numina than disciplines as such. But it's still there, and thin-blooded are still around regardless how they function mechanically separate than full-blooded vampires, so here we are.<br /> <br /> Even as such, this is somewhat of a skewed statement as the core book only includes half the clans...or every discipline. &quot;Having to pay&quot; for Hecata, BH, Ministry, Lasombra, or Oblivion be damned. The V5 Companion includes the Ravnos, Salubri, and Tzimisce, and is entirely free. That reduces the &quot;unique disciplines anyway&quot; number down to two, given Tzimisce have Protean in v5.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...The biggest advocate against them unironically told me without necromancy the giovanni...Then turned around and defended clan tremere... </div> </div> </div>Yes yes, past edition blood sorcery is gonzo Mickey Mouse bullshit, we all know it and admit it. Other than the fact if you're playing a dedicated blood sorcerer, you're blowing your load in a bottomless experience point toilet for diminishing returns, severe deficiencies in every other discipline, and having to spend every solo scene and downtime horse trading, stealing, or killing for rituals and secondary paths. And most of the time, all while fighting for your character's unlife.<br /> <br /> It makes me weep the bitterest tears of empathy for all those who needlessly suffer; it's hard to fathom how difficult it must be to just write &quot;Brujah&quot; or &quot;Lasombra&quot; on a character sheet. I can only imagine how humiliating it must be to ask the player next to you for extra dice because you didn't bring enough to roll damage on your fourth attack in the round, or need the storyteller to keep track of which tentacles are killing who while the character's safely out of combat.<br /> <br /> But silly me, what would I know. I only played a Tremere through the entirety of Transylvania Chronicles and managed to survive it.<br /> <br /> But we were talking about the Giovanni, weren't we?<br /> <br /> The usurper clan which ate its way to power, and manufactured consent for it through a clanwide defamation campaign? no, wait, that's the Tremere.<br /> <br /> The clan who maintains strong mortal familial ties, and uses selective inbreeding to create ideal Embrace candidates and even has a revenant family? shit, that's the Tzimisce.<br /> <br /> The clan who maintains a facade of piety while manipulating the Catholic church for power, and openly mocks it amongst their own? dangit, that's the Lasombra.<br /> <br /> Oh, I know. The mob clan! ...damn, I'm really getting my clans mixed up tonight, that's the Ventrue. Renaissance merchants? no, that was <i>definitely</i> the Ventrue, too.<br /> <br /> They're Italian? that can't be right, that's the Ventrue <i>and</i> the Lasombra.<br /> <br /> They're...serial necrophiliacs? okay, yeah, they got <i>that</i> going for them. Congratulations!<br /> <br /> So, what the hell are the Giovanni anyway? It's just me, but it sounds a <i>little bit</i> like they're one of those &quot;cultural stereotype with a proprietary discipline&quot; splats White Wolf loved so much in the early to mid '90s. And everything retconned into the Giovanni to give them a semblance of character and lore since, is giving them another clan's hat. Which would make them about the single worst example in the entirety of World of Darkness to use as a defense for the position clans should have hats.<br /> <br /> I mean, Tremere may be dumb but they at least have <i>some</i> things unique about them, and the entirety of their lore and clan character spins off the unifying aspect of being a usurper clan and the long-term ramifications of it. Right up to the point their crippling over-centralization led to their being reduced to a glorified bloodline, thanks to a single bunker-busting guided missile.<br /> <br /> Anyhow, moving on...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...V5 Oblivion's... </div> </div> </div>Yeah, there aren't any &quot;awkward contortions&quot; there, and nor is it a shoehorn.<br /> <br /> Obtenebration's been written as Abyss-derived since revised edition, and the one thing internally consistent about WoD's cosmology across editions is the Abyss is the lowest level of the Umbra. Different fatplsats may call it different names, but it's the same place. That's why wraiths and spectres go bonkers around Lasombra and uses of Obtenebration to a degree only seen elsewhere among necromancers, and why &quot;shadow creatures&quot; and &quot;Abyssal manifestations&quot; are explicitly described in terms that identify them to any other fatsplat as Umbrood.<br /> <br /> White Wolf was, at that point, still reticent about forcing players to buy other games' supplements just to understand the one they're currently playing. Which is why the Abyss isn't explicitly called out as the same realm as Oblivion, the Chasm, or Maw of the Void (or whatever the fuck it was called in WtA), in Vampire books. They were content about being tongue-in-cheek about it, using the same language and &quot;merely&quot; <i>very strongly implying</i> that was the case in every last supplement. Us MtAs players didn't have it quite so good.<br /> <br /> Hence Abyss Mysticism, which you'll note is an Obtenebration-derived form of blood sorcery which specifically draws further on the power of the Abyss. You'll further note at least two Oblivion powers (because Oblivion Sight and Maw of Ahriman are the two I can name off the top of my head) are <i>Abyss Mysticism</i> powers, not Obtenebration proper, Mortis, <i>or</i> Necromancy.<br /> <br /> This isn't a retcon, and it certainly isn't a sudden or unexpected shift in either game mechanics, worldbuilding, or lore. Unifying Obenebration and Necromancy was the culmination of groundwork laid by White Wolf twenty-five years ago.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 01-14-2024, 12:58 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-14T03:01:26-08:00","id":1528529,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1528524#post1528524\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's true in every edition, in which the core rulebook only includes the original seven Camarilla clans. And in this case, it's not even factually correct given Caitiff are in the core rulebook and are explicitly by RAW capable of starting with any discipline. Even taking Caitiff out of the question as I'm sure for some reason they &quot;won't count&quot;, Protean isn't even unique as two of the ten predator types in the book give a free dot of Protean...and one of those two is the predator type that gives you a free dot of blood potency to boot.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DHO8gtfowN8\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DHO8gtfowN8\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DHO8gtfowN8\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DHO8gtfowN8 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> 3 out of 5 edition books as I recall, There's very little preventing you from taking a unique discipline at character creation, all you really need is GM approval and the expend the starter bonus points so this isnt much of a defense.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That brings the number of &quot;unique disciplines anyway&quot; down to...one. I hesitate to even include TBA on a list of disciplines, as it functions far more like numina than disciplines as such. But it's still there, and thin-blooded are still around regardless how they function mechanically separate than full-blooded vampires, so here we are. </div> </div> </div>No...it keeps it at 3 as previously discussed, we're discussing unique clan disciplines inherently built into clans not rules you can use to circumvent that limitation. Also your description of TBA really hammers home how gimmicky it is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Even as such, this is somewhat of a skewed statement as the core book only includes half the clans...or every discipline. &quot;Having to pay&quot; for Hecata, BH, Ministry, Lasombra, or Oblivion be damned. The V5 Companion includes the Ravnos, Salubri, and Tzimisce, and is entirely free. That reduces the &quot;unique disciplines anyway&quot; number down to two, given Tzimisce have Protean in v5. </div> </div> </div>That doesn't really relate to my observation, my observation is if you're running pure corebook then their are in fact 3 unique disciplines straight out the stable. Now later books do amend this....after 2 or anarch with a 40 quid price tag years but my point still stands.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes yes, past edition blood sorcery is gonzo Mickey Mouse bullshit, we all know it and admit it. Other than the fact if you're playing a dedicated blood sorcerer, you're blowing your load in a bottomless experience point toilet for diminishing returns, severe deficiencies in every other discipline, and having to spend every solo scene and downtime horse trading, stealing, or killing for rituals and secondary paths. And most of the time, all while fighting for your character's unlife.<br /> <br /> It makes me weep the bitterest tears of empathy for all those who needlessly suffer; it's hard to fathom how difficult it must be to just write &quot;Brujah&quot; or &quot;Lasombra&quot; on a character sheet. I can only imagine how humiliating it must be to ask the player next to you for extra dice because you didn't bring enough to roll damage on your fourth attack in the round, or need the storyteller to keep track of which tentacles are killing who while the character's safely out of combat.<br /> <br /> But silly me, what would I know. I only played a Tremere through the entirety of Transylvania Chronicles and managed to survive it. </div> </div> </div>Well most of this is a strawman so can be dismissed out of hand but your style of writing and approach to people who disagree with you is instructive in how spiteful you come across, this is oddly a common trend I've noticed in the anti unique discipline camp. Every one who likes Serpantis is a colossal prick out to personally ruin your game apparently.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <br /> But we were talking about the Giovanni, weren't we?<br /> <br /> The usurper clan which ate its way to power, and manufactured consent for it through a clanwide defamation campaign? no, wait, that's the Tremere.<br /> <br /> The clan who maintains strong mortal familial ties, and uses selective inbreeding to create ideal Embrace candidates and even has a revenant family? shit, that's the Tzimisce.<br /> <br /> The clan who maintains a facade of piety while manipulating the Catholic church for power, and openly mocks it amongst their own? dangit, that's the Lasombra.<br /> <br /> Oh, I know. The mob clan! ...damn, I'm really getting my clans mixed up tonight, that's the Ventrue. Renaissance merchants? no, that was <i>definitely</i> the Ventrue, too.<br /> <br /> They're Italian? that can't be right, that's the Ventrue <i>and</i> the Lasombra.<br /> <br /> They're...serial necrophiliacs? okay, yeah, they got <i>that</i> going for them. Congratulations! </div> </div> </div>You can be very reductive about pretty any clan, sitting here I was able to draw enough comparisons to imply clan toreador are just ripping off bits of Nosferatu/brujah/ventrue. It's not a particularly difficult thing to do and presumably you'd have explanations on why that doesn't count.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, what the hell are the Giovanni anyway? It's just me, but it sounds a <i>little bit</i> like they're one of those &quot;cultural stereotype with a proprietary discipline&quot; splats White Wolf loved so much in the early to mid '90s. And everything retconned into the Giovanni to give them a semblance of character and lore since, is giving them another clan's hat. Which would make them about the single worst example in the entirety of World of Darkness to use as a defense for the position clans should have hats. </div> </div> </div>lol no, to my recollection venetians arnt typically stereotyped as necromancers engaged in absolute depravity, I suppose you could consider the mercantile origin a historical nod. 3rd edition kind of screw them slightly up since justin seems keen on the idea of them as mafia but that's more a fundamental misconception of the clan, I'm also slightly bemused at your assumption that any of the clans don't initially start out as a limited archetype. go read 1 ed introductions and take note how one note they initially are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I mean, Tremere may be dumb but they at least have <i>some</i> things unique about them, and the entirety of their lore and clan character spins off the unifying aspect of being a usurper clan and the long-term ramifications of it. Right up to the point their crippling over-centralization led to their being reduced to a glorified bloodline, thanks to a single bunker-busting guided missile </div> </div> </div>Well the tremere arnt a dumb clan, they're really interesting and instructive in how far you can go with a simple concept (clan wizard) and build on it and implicitly undermine the whole idea that having a clan strongly associate with it's power is a limitation in terms of writing. As a side your flippant style of writing makes it hard to read if you've even got a point are you arguing the Tremere only became a well written clan once vienna fell?<br /> <br /> <br /> The next section wasnt written for me but since i'm here....<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yeah, there aren't any &quot;awkward contortions&quot; there, and nor is it a shoehorn. </div> </div> </div>It's pretty awkward, mechanically obtenerbration works nothing like necromancy in previous editions and nothing about the Lasombra really overlapped with the necromancer clans. Their's also a dearth of plot holes required for no one to notice the lasombra were just practicing specialized necromancy for 4000 years.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Obtenebration's been written as Abyss-derived since revised edition, and the one thing internally consistent about WoD's cosmology across editions is the Abyss is the lowest level of the Umbra. Different fatplsats may call it different names, but it's the same place. That's why wraiths and spectres go bonkers around Lasombra and uses of Obtenebration to a degree only seen elsewhere among necromancers, and why &quot;shadow creatures&quot; and &quot;Abyssal manifestations&quot; are explicitly described in terms that identify them to any other fatsplat as Umbrood. </div> </div> </div>Theirs some indication the Abyss is Oblivion in some books but it isnt really confirmed, for example clan Lasombra revised and 20th vtm doesn't really draw that comparison at all. Generally the more common characterization is almost lovecraftian which the underworld explicitly isn't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> White Wolf was, at that point, still reticent about forcing players to buy other games' supplements just to understand the one they're currently playing. Which is why the Abyss isn't explicitly called out as the same realm as Oblivion, the Chasm, or Maw of the Void (or whatever the fuck it was called in WtA), in Vampire books. They were content about being tongue-in-cheek about it, using the same language and &quot;merely&quot; <i>very strongly implying</i> that was the case in every last supplement. Us MtAs players didn't have it quite so good. </div> </div> </div>Do you actually need to understand the abyss in the neatly defined cosmology sense? If anything id' probably argue it's more fun not to know or build in some far worse revelation than ob is just extra spooky necromancy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hence Abyss Mysticism, which you'll note is an Obtenebration-derived form of blood sorcery which specifically draws further on the power of the Abyss. You'll further note at least two Oblivion powers (because Oblivion Sight and Maw of Ahriman are the two I can name off the top of my head) are <i>Abyss Mysticism</i> powers, not Obtenebration proper, Mortis, <i>or</i> Necromancy. </div> </div> </div>Yes hence why it feels clumsy and forced, you've got an obviously distinct power set clumsily wedged in.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This isn't a retcon, and it certainly isn't a sudden or unexpected shift in either game mechanics, worldbuilding, or lore. Unifying Obenebration and Necromancy was the culmination of groundwork laid by White Wolf twenty-five years ago. </div> </div> </div>it is a retcon, and it being something laid out beforehand doesn't change that it's also editorial mandate and obviously so. Everything has to be neat and if the Heceta get to keep their unique disciplines then this is apparently unacceptable because nobody else did.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 01-14-2024, 02:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8},{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-14T10:11:00-08:00","id":1528547,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar431_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"431\" data-vbnamecard=\"431\">Theodrim</a>, lemme ask you: so is [Lasombra] ruling part of this specific Abyss? I mean, the Fallen, Black Spiral Dancers, Shadows, Giovanni, and Lasombra are constantly crossing in their dealings with the Abyss? Oh, and Umbrood don't used to referr only to the Abyss natives, but to the whole Umbra. Is so Umbrood a tree spirit as is Lucifer and the Wyrm.<br /> Now, what I understand of the old Abyss: just another Umbral kingdom, based on the darkness. As all the other inimaginable numbers of Umbral kingdoms, shard realms, shade realms, astral reaches and everything else that populated the Umbrae.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27825-mrdrika","name":"MrDrika","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27825_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"64"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-14T11:52:16-08:00","id":1528554,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1528529#post1528529\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> 3 out of 5 edition books as I recall...</div> </div> </div> </div>V5 isn't one of those editions, but for predator types. Which are RAW, especially written to facilitate this decision point in character generation, and serve the specific purpose to diversify characters in accordance to <i>characterization</i> as opposed to predetermined faction and splat choice.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...we're discussing unique clan disciplines inherently built into clans not rules you can use to circumvent that limitation... </div> </div> </div>In other words, you only want to discuss what &quot;proves&quot; your point, opposed to RAW which even under your arbitrary and cherry-picked criteria still disproves your point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also your description of TBA really hammers home how gimmicky it is. </div> </div> </div>Agreed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well most of this is a strawman... </div> </div> </div>Appeal to authority, if we're going to pick nits. The authority that stems from nearly thirty years' experience playing and running VtM, where most of that experience as a player comes from playing blood sorcerers of multiple clans and bloodlines, while the clan I play most is actually <i>Lasombra</i>. That puts me in a position to know <i>exactly</i> where blood sorcery stands in game balance terms, especially compared to disciplines like Obtenebration and Celerity (with or without Potence).<br /> <br /> And that's exactly why I decided to indulge a bit of spite with this response: it's long been my observation those overly critical of blood sorcery from a game balance standpoint, tend to have quite the blind spot when it comes to disciplines that directly interact with action economy, dice pools, and/or target numbers. It even extends so far as their own criticisms of blood sorcery, as when critics are asked what they feel to be the most problematic blood sorcery paths, Focused Mind rarely if ever earns a spot on the list. Focused Mind is unquestionably the most overpowered blood sorcery path in the game, because of its interactions with action economy, dice pools, and target numbers; that's all the path does, in fact.<br /> <br /> Or to put it another way, Shroud of Night and Arms of Ahriman/Darksteel get the pass, as do basically any level of combined Celerity and Potence. But as soon as a character starts picking up dots in secondary paths and rituals regardless of what those paths and rituals actually do or what it costs to get them, it's time to break out the torches and pitchforks.<br /> <br /> Despite how powerful action economy is in VtM, and powers that grant action economy are exponentially more toxic to a chronicle's long-term health than any path or ritual. Despite that Shroud of Night and Arms of Ahriman are accessible from character generation, as is Cel/Potence to Brujah, while it takes at least a hundred accrued experience (or more) sunk into nothing but blood sorcery, just for it to <i>start</i> having &quot;problematic&quot; effects on gameplay.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You can be very reductive about pretty any clan... </div> </div> </div>You certainly can, but only in few instances can one be reductive to the point of identifying a cultural stereotype with a proprietary discipline. The Giovanni happen to be one of those, and that's the one you picked in the argument clans should have &quot;hats&quot;. It would have been the same had you mentioned BH, Ravnos, or Setites. But being this was originally about Setites in the first place, it hardly needs to be restated.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...3rd edition kind of screw them slightly up since justin seems keen on the idea of them as mafia but that's more a fundamental misconception of the clan... </div> </div> </div>Your perspective on what is a retcon and what isn't, is rather interesting and seems to neatly coincide to the points you're trying to make.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well the tremere arnt a dumb clan... </div> </div> </div>No, they're a dumb clan. If no one else is willing to out with it, I will. And I say that as someone who loves the Tremere, despite how dumb they are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> , they're really interesting and instructive in how far you can go with a simple concept (clan wizard) and build on it and implicitly undermine the whole idea that having a clan strongly associate with it's power is a limitation in terms of writing. </div> </div> </div>Well, here's the question at the heart of the subject. Are the Tremere a totalitarian personality cult of wizards who resorted to paranoid doomsday prepping as an overreaction to their origins, or are they a totalitarian personality cult of paranoid doomsday preppers who also happen to be blood wizards?<br /> <br /> You want to complain about my reductionism with regards to clans, but right there it is. Tremere actually aren't based on a simple, single concept of &quot;wizard clan&quot;. Just as every other original clan satirized some aspect of '80s and '90s culture and counter-culture, so too did the Tremere: doomsday cults and militias. Even the real world Victorian occultist movements which were the inspiration for the Tremere's aesthetics, were deep into eschatology.<br /> <br /> This is where the Tremere differ from Giovanni, and apparently also the Setites (I vehemently disagree, but this isn't about <i>my</i> point of view): one could actually remove Thaumaturgy from the Tremere's discipline spread and replace it with any other, and they'd still be the Tremere. You wouldn't have to ask, &quot;why does this clan even exist?&quot; They'd still be a totalitarian personality cult of paranoid doomsday preppers, separate and distinct from the Nosferatu (who aren't a totalitarian personality cult despite being paranoid doomsday preppers), Ventrue (who are a totalitarian personality cult, but not paranoid doomsday preppers), or Malkavians (because paranoid doomsday prepping is <i>quite</i> sane when there are hungry, sleeping Lovecraftian monstrosities around).<br /> <br /> Giovanni without Necromancy? why aren't these just a Ventrue or Lasombra bloodline? White Wolf had to give the Ventrue's organized crime hat to them, just to justify their existence. Which didn't do much more than double down on the cultural stereotype aspect to their characterization in the end.<br /> <br /> Setites? the fact this entire kerfluffle originated from people taking exception to the removal of Serpentis says more than enough about it. White Wolf had to give the Tremere's cult hat to them, just to justify their existence. Which didn't do much more than double down on the cultural stereotype aspect to their characterization in the end.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As a side your flippant style of writing makes it hard to read if you've even got a point are you arguing the Tremere only became a well written clan once vienna fell? </div> </div> </div>Well, <i>yeah</i>. Game books have been foreshadowing the Tremere's expiration date is faster approaching than others' since revised, going so far as to directly hint the Tremere's crippling centralization will be their undoing. It's precisely how the Tremere go out in four out of the five published Gehenna scenarios, if I remember right.<br /> <br /> Their doomsday came. Prepping not only failed to work, it made them <i>vulnerable </i>to doomsday. Now they're a clan in diaspora, trying to pick up the pieces and get their shit together before a millennium's worth of karmic debt comes due, while a whole bunch of them think running far away and never looking back will save them. That's a lot more interesting, with greater opportunity for compelling story hooks and character arcs, than paranoid doomsday prepping.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Their's also a dearth of plot holes required for no one to notice the lasombra were just practicing specialized necromancy for 4000 years. </div> </div> </div>In about the same way the Cappadocian's eradication was a plot hole, how Mortis was nearly lost was a plot hole, how the Roads devolved to Paths was a plot hole, and why few to no koldunic sorcerers survive in modern nights was a plot hole. In this case, it was the Lasombra eating <i>themselves</i> stupid in the Anarch revolt.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Theirs some indication the Abyss is Oblivion in some books but it isnt really confirmed, for example clan Lasombra revised and 20th vtm doesn't really draw that comparison at all. </div> </div> </div>Well, was Onyx Path planning to write and publish WtO 20th Anniversary at the time, or were they trying to keep VtM 20th Anniversary a standalone product? You might want to check out what DAV20 has to say about it, because that was published <i>after</i> VtM 20th was successful enough to push the full 20th Anniversary line.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Generally the more common characterization is almost lovecraftian which the underworld explicitly isn't. </div> </div> </div>Well, that's good because the Underworld isn't the Abyss, or even the Labyrinth.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> it is a retcon, and it being something laid out beforehand doesn't change that it's also editorial mandate and obviously so. </div> </div> </div>Again with the &quot;it's only a retcon if I don't like it&quot; thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Everything has to be neat and if the Heceta get to keep their unique disciplines then this is apparently unacceptable because nobody else did. </div> </div> </div>The irony of this, is the one and only Oblivion power (or Oblivion ceremony) that's Hecata-only by RAW is Fortezza Sindonica, the wraith-only version of Warding Circle against Spirits. Which means it's not even truly unique. But the Cult of Shalim gets three, the Sabbat get two, and as such aren't even Lasombra-only. Naw, that whopping <i>one</i> power just throws the entire feng shui off.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-14T12:06:10-08:00","id":1528557,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1528524#post1528524\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, there aren't any &quot;awkward contortions&quot; there, and nor is it a shoehorn.<br /> <br /> Obtenebration's been written as Abyss-derived since revised edition, and the one thing internally consistent about WoD's cosmology across editions is the Abyss is the lowest level of the Umbra. Different fatplsats may call it different names, but it's the same place. That's why wraiths and spectres go bonkers around Lasombra and uses of Obtenebration to a degree only seen elsewhere among necromancers, and why &quot;shadow creatures&quot; and &quot;Abyssal manifestations&quot; are explicitly described in terms that identify them to any other fatsplat as Umbrood.<br /> <br /> White Wolf was, at that point, still reticent about forcing players to buy other games' supplements just to understand the one they're currently playing. Which is why the Abyss isn't explicitly called out as the same realm as Oblivion, the Chasm, or Maw of the Void (or whatever the fuck it was called in WtA), in Vampire books. They were content about being tongue-in-cheek about it, using the same language and &quot;merely&quot; <i>very strongly implying</i> that was the case in every last supplement. Us MtAs players didn't have it quite so good.<br /> <br /> Hence Abyss Mysticism, which you'll note is an Obtenebration-derived form of blood sorcery which specifically draws further on the power of the Abyss. You'll further note at least two Oblivion powers (because Oblivion Sight and Maw of Ahriman are the two I can name off the top of my head) are <i>Abyss Mysticism</i> powers, not Obtenebration proper, Mortis, <i>or</i> Necromancy.<br /> <br /> This isn't a retcon, and it certainly isn't a sudden or unexpected shift in either game mechanics, worldbuilding, or lore. Unifying Obenebration and Necromancy was the culmination of groundwork laid by White Wolf twenty-five years ago.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with Ragged Robin and MrDrika that the Abyss of the Lasombra, the 'Abyss' of the Tempest, and Abyss in which the Fallen were bound seem to have notable differences in terms of what you're likely to find in them and themes associated with each. Obtenebration and the Abyss, as Ragged Robbin noted, tend to be conceptualized as darkness beyond known creation rather than locations within/emanations of the Underworld, and the denizens found within it have no connections to wraiths or spectres, which would have been easy enough to establish within the context of VtM if desired. The closest, and really only, thematic tie between the Abyss and the Underworld that comes to mind is through horrors of pre-creation that the Neverborn may represent, but the Neverborn don't seem to have any particular interactions with Abyssal denizens or the Lasombra, and even keeping in mind the desire to avoid cross-splat dependence it'd be easy to refer to some primeval force or influence within the depths of the Underworld. Furthermore, the fact that neither Obtenebration nor Abyss Mysticism have any stated interactions with the Shroud suggests against Underworld connections, as does the fact that Lasombra have used the Abyss as a meeting ground for ages without developing any particular familiarity or relationships with spectres or the like. Even if the groundwork has been laid for a while, it hasn't been laid very well.<br /> <br /> But that wasn't even what I was really talking about when I brought up Oblivion. The awkward contortions and shoehorning I'm referring to concern the way Oblivion as a Discipline is set up. Even though they decided to mash Necromancy and Obtenebration together as a 'Dark Arts' Discipline in the most generic sense, they clearly also wanted to disincentivize shadow-bending Hecata and necromancer Lasombra; hence the introduction of power prerequisites for Oblivion Ceremonies and the need to use whatever was on hand for them regardless of well or poorly it fit. This makes Oblivion more invested in separating its internal 'tracks' than any other Discipline, and leads me to question whether it was really worth bringing them together at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","name":"Paramecius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27894_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-15T10:11:59-08:00","id":1528599,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1528554#post1528554\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">V5 isn't one of those editions, but for predator types. Which are RAW, especially written to facilitate this decision point in character generation, and serve the specific purpose to diversify characters in accordance to <i>characterization</i> as opposed to predetermined faction and splat choice.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah it is you can pick protean using a char gen gimmick despite it being a unique clan dicipline in core book. much like you can do it with freebie points in all other editions.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In other words, you only want to discuss what &quot;proves&quot; your point, opposed to RAW which even under your arbitrary and cherry-picked criteria still disproves your point. </div> </div> </div>Well yes i do tend to use sources and examples which back up my point, that's generally how you argue things. for example if we model your argument that you can use char gen rules to get protean which is a unique clan discipline so doesn't count as a unique clan discipline. However you can simply purchase in previous editions any discipline with freebie points ergo their are no unique disciplines under your logic correct?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Appeal to authority, if we're going to pick nits. The authority that stems from nearly thirty years' experience playing and running VtM, where most of that experience as a player comes from playing blood sorcerers of multiple clans and bloodlines, while the clan I play most is actually <i>Lasombra</i>. That puts me in a position to know <i>exactly</i> where blood sorcery stands in game balance terms, especially compared to disciplines like Obtenebration and Celerity (with or without Potence). </div> </div> </div>I don't really consider you an authority in any meaningful context: I have around 20 under my belt (ugh) and your write up of your Tremere play experience, salt and takes challenges that notion. But I'd assumed it was a hypothetical because of the absurdity of the anecdote. apologies.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And that's exactly why I decided to indulge a bit of spite with this response: it's long been my observation those overly critical of blood sorcery from a game balance standpoint, tend to have quite the blind spot when it comes to disciplines that directly interact with action economy, dice pools, and/or target numbers. It even extends so far as their own criticisms of blood sorcery, as when critics are asked what they feel to be the most problematic blood sorcery paths, Focused Mind rarely if ever earns a spot on the list. Focused Mind is unquestionably the most overpowered blood sorcery path in the game, because of its interactions with action economy, dice pools, and target numbers; that's all the path does, in fact.<br /> ss<br /> Or to put it another way, Shroud of Night and Arms of Ahriman/Darksteel get the pass, as do basically any level of combined Celerity and Potence. But as soon as a character starts picking up dots in secondary paths and rituals regardless of what those paths and rituals actually do or what it costs to get them, it's time to break out the torches and pitchforks. </div> </div> </div>I'm sorry but I'm not accountable for your experiences with bad faith role players and it's an absurd idea to try and structure the game around them otherwise I want clan Brujah gone.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You certainly can, but only in few instances can one be reductive to the point of identifying a cultural stereotype with a proprietary discipline. The Giovanni happen to be one of those, and that's the one you picked in the argument clans should have &quot;hats&quot;. It would have been the same had you mentioned BH, Ravnos, or Setites. But being this was originally about Setites in the first place, it hardly needs to be restated. </div> </div> </div>This is a bad framework and in some cases categorically false anyway, The setite discipline doesn't really have much to do with what they do in terms of vampire ecology and culture being more a motiff and nod to conan, if they had a discipline which reflected the clan it'd probably be blood sorcery or maybe auspex due to their mystic streak, since you're claiming 30 years you probably remember this being a talking point back in the day. The Giovanni are heavily influenced by the necromantic aspects of the culture but to be candid-that's a hell of a hat, The Ravnos? Ehhh kinda but you could easily replace chim with obfuscate and they'd basically be the same so it isn't really much of a hat.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your perspective on what is a retcon and what isn't, is rather interesting and seems to neatly coincide to the points you're trying to make. </div> </div> </div>Well yes.....because I'm correct. But no the mafia thing isnt a retcon, it's added on later and isnt in direct contradiction to previous lore. It is bad writting but it isnt a retcon.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No, they're a dumb clan. If no one else is willing to out with it, I will. And I say that as someone who loves the Tremere, despite how dumb they are. </div> </div> </div>lol k especially as you're description bellow kinda buries this idea<br /> <br /> .<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Giovanni without Necromancy? why aren't these just a Ventrue or Lasombra bloodline? White Wolf had to give the Ventrue's organized crime hat to them, just to justify their existence. Which didn't do much more than double down on the cultural stereotype aspect to their characterization in the end. </div> </div> </div>Well the Ventrue and lasombra are very much into organized crime, most clans are so again sloppy lazy reductionalism as well as an odd gap in knowledgue for a 30 year player. In terms of justifying their existence-they combine a large number of traits to unique effects, all clans have overlap but each is unique in the naunces and finer details. Necromancy is a big part of the clan yes but how that integrates into their larger culture is frankly fascinating.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Setites? the fact this entire kerfluffle originated from people taking exception to the removal of Serpentis says more than enough about it. White Wolf had to give the Tremere's cult hat to them, just to justify their existence. Which didn't do much more than double down on the cultural stereotype aspect to their characterization in the end. </div> </div> </div>Their isn't much top say here, I'm not even sure who the setites are supposed to be stereotyping (ancient coptics who lost the power struggle with the horus priests?) once again hyper reductive the setites arn't particularly similar to the Tremere.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, <i>yeah</i>. Game books have been foreshadowing the Tremere's expiration date is faster approaching than others' since revised, going so far as to directly hint the Tremere's crippling centralization will be their undoing. It's precisely how the Tremere go out in four out of the five published Gehenna scenarios, if I remember right. </div> </div> </div>As I recall Gehenna goes well for a grand number of 0 clans, although i never liked the book anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Their doomsday came. Prepping not only failed to work, it made them <i>vulnerable </i>to doomsday. Now they're a clan in diaspora, trying to pick up the pieces and get their shit together before a millennium's worth of karmic debt comes due, while a whole bunch of them think running far away and never looking back will save them. That's a lot more interesting, with greater opportunity for compelling story hooks and character arcs, than paranoid doomsday prepping. </div> </div> </div>It's not purely prepping it's more a general siege mentality, they've had 800 (i think) years of it working because unified cohesive organizations are generally more effective. Which is pretty interesting even before the drone strike on a number of levels.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In about the same way the Cappadocian's eradication was a plot hole, how Mortis was nearly lost was a plot hole, how the Roads devolved to Paths was a plot hole, and why few to no koldunic sorcerers survive in modern nights was a plot hole. In this case, it was the Lasombra eating <i>themselves</i> stupid in the Anarch revolt. </div> </div> </div>Yeah I really hate the way the anarch revolt is often treated a giant plot device to cover up the cracks but it goes way beyond that, I can think of far more issues of it sitting here slightly bored.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, was Onyx Path planning to write and publish WtO 20th Anniversary at the time, or were they trying to keep VtM 20th Anniversary a standalone product? You might want to check out what DAV20 has to say about it, because that was published <i>after</i> VtM 20th was successful enough to push the full 20th Anniversary line. </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure how that contradicts my point<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, that's good because the Underworld isn't the Abyss, or even the Labyrinth. </div> </div> </div>Correct that's why I said Underworld, their are parts of wraith that are arguably elderich but they're not lovecraftian.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Again with the &quot;it's only a retcon if I don't like it&quot; thing. </div> </div> </div>Well their are good retcons out their (Cappadocious being insane, Fraiser not being an only child etc) but a long run retcon is still a retcon and ultimately it's still bad writing as a whole in this case.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The irony of this, is the one and only Oblivion power (or Oblivion ceremony) that's Hecata-only by RAW is Fortezza Sindonica, the wraith-only version of Warding Circle against Spirits. Which means it's not even truly unique. But the Cult of Shalim gets three, the Sabbat get two, and as such aren't even Lasombra-only. Naw, that whopping <i>one</i> power just throws the entire feng shui off. </div> </div> </div>Okay....and if I'm reading you right your largely agreeing with my comment about oblivion not being a unique discipline because for reasons it's unacceptable for the lasombra and heceta to have that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 01-15-2024, 02:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-15T11:55:33-08:00","id":1528615,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Paramecius</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1528557#post1528557\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> ....the Abyss of the Lasombra, the 'Abyss' of the Tempest, and Abyss in which the Fallen were bound seem to have notable differences...</div> </div> </div> </div>I just explained this. Those realms have notable differences, because they <i>are</i> different realms. What you're describing is the <i>Labyrinth</i>, and realms vertically parallel to Oblivion but accessible from the Labyrinth, not Oblivion itself. Those are separate and distinct realms within the Low Umbra, much as how Malfeas is vertically parallel to the Labyrinth and accessible from it, but horizontally deeper. They just happen to be <i>called</i> different things by fatsplats, because those fatsplats' understanding of the Umbra varies and is contingent upon their relationship to it.<br /> <br /> Again, this is confused by writers' desire to keep Vampire relatively self-contained unlike other game lines like MtAs, which constantly refers players to WtO and WtA for lore and context on the Umbra and its various realms (and vice versa when applicable). Such as for instance in older editions when Koldun interacted with Banes and could create fomori, and Kupala's subordinate spirits had Bane-specific charms, with storytellers being directed to Book of the Wyrm and Freak Legion for more information and mechanics -- all without outright calling them Banes or fomori.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...The closest, and really only, thematic tie between the Abyss and the Underworld that comes to mind is through horrors of pre-creation that the Neverborn may represent, but the Neverborn don't seem to have any particular interactions with Abyssal denizens or the Lasombra... </div> </div> </div>Here, you're outright conflating Oblivion with the Labyrinth. The Neverborn and Malfeans are explicitly and unambiguously stated to be denizens of the Labyrinth.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Furthermore, the fact that neither Obtenebration nor Abyss Mysticism have any stated interactions with the Shroud suggests against Underworld connections, as does the fact that Lasombra have used the Abyss as a meeting ground... </div> </div> </div>They don't have to if their discipline allows for bypassing the Underworld and/or Labyrinth entirely, and connecting the Skinlands directly to Oblivion. Which is precisely what several (albeit elder-level) Obtenebration powers actually do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Even if the groundwork has been laid for a while, it hasn't been laid very well. </div> </div> </div>It has been -- one just needs to understand the cosmology in the context no individual fatsplat is omniscient first.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...hence the introduction of power prerequisites for Oblivion Ceremonies and the need to use whatever was on hand for them regardless of well or poorly it fit. This makes Oblivion more invested in separating its internal 'tracks' than any other Discipline, and leads me to question whether it was really worth bringing them together at all. </div> </div> </div>So, what about Oblivion powers that have prerequisites?<br /> <br /> If this was really the design intent, wouldn't Touch of Oblivion have Ashes to Ashes as a prerequisite? They're adaptations of Grave's Decay and/or Mortis powers, depending on edition. Surely Arms of Ahriman's prerequisite would be Shadow Cloak or Oblivion Sight? You'd think Tenebrous Avatar would have literally any prerequisite at all let alone an &quot;Obtenebration&quot; prerequisite, but a vampire that took nothing but &quot;Mortis&quot; and/or &quot;Necromancy&quot; powers from levels 1-4 can acquire it as their capstone just fine, regardless of clan, bloodline, sect, or cult.<br /> <br /> It's really telling that in one post your complaint was that Hecata apparently get to keep their unique powers, and when it was pointed out to you they actually didn't, now your complaint is...ceremonies having power prerequisites is a sign of clan propriety. Except, Oblivion <i>powers</i> don't have prerequisites, either. Just amalgams.<br /> <br /> The irony of this? Shadow Servant, a <i>level 3</i> Oblivion power, is an adaptation of a <i>level 1</i> Abyss Mysticism ritual. It also happens to be an <i>Auspex</i> amalgam, which by your own logic implies propriety to Hecata (because it's &quot;easier to get as Auspex is innate to Hecata&quot; or whatever). Right here's the example you're grasping at straws to find, yet you haven't mentioned it once. Why might that be?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":6},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-15T14:04:44-08:00","id":1528622,"title":"","content":"Now [Lasombra] is a conscious part of the OBLIVION and facing reaction of their denizens? And the Amici Noctis go to Oblivion to do planning and controlling the clan? I mean, wouldn't be more sensible a Umbral realm instead of the (now very populated) Oblivion? And of course, if the Lasombras are manipulating the energies of Oblivion, not anything else, literally anything else, their power is lethal. Remembering that before being a name of a discipline, the Oblivion is the final, total and utterly destruction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27825-mrdrika","name":"MrDrika","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27825_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"64"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-15T17:52:05-08:00","id":1528630,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1528615#post1528615\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here, you're outright conflating Oblivion with the Labyrinth. The Neverborn and Malfeans are explicitly and unambiguously stated to be denizens of the Labyrinth.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not really. I mentioned the Underworld, which according to the <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Underworld_(WOD)\" target=\"_blank\">wiki</a> (Wraith isn't my game so that's what I'm going on until I see some better sources) is comprised of various regions including Oblivion; the comparison to the Neverborn was based on what I saw as the closest point of thematic commonality between features of the Underworld and features of the Abyss that the Lasombra tap into.<br /> <br /> The conceptual distance only grows if Oblivion itself is outright supposed to be Abyss. Firstly and most fundamentally, this is because Oblivion is entropy and destruction: the only sense in which it encompasses darkness is as the death of light. That's plenty for the literal definition of darkness, but that isn't what Obtenebration trades in: darkness as an elemental force unto itself that largely derives its destructive potential from its growing physical tangibility, as evidenced by Arms of the Abyss and Black Metamorphosis.<br /> <br /> If Obtenebration derives from Oblivion, a more coherent progression of its capabilities be along the lines of actualizing ambient entropic potential: making flickering lights gutter out rather than molding darkness like clay, exacerbating wounds and diseases rather than battering and choking with shadow tentacles, destabilizing substance rather than merging with darkness, etc. Lending this sort of tangibility to nothingness is the sort of quasi-paradoxical feat that would suggest mastery rather than mid-range competence as with Obtenebration, whereas the only point at which Obtenebration can directly unmake things in V20 is with near-Antedeluvian levels of power (Ahriman's Demesne).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They don't have to if their discipline allows for bypassing the Underworld and/or Labyrinth entirely, and connecting the Skinlands directly to Oblivion. Which is precisely what several (albeit elder-level) Obtenebration powers actually do. </div> </div> </div>If Oblivion is in fact part of the Underworld as the wiki states, the Shroud applies to it as well, barring extraordinary effects (such as elder-level Discipline powers) that can breach or bypass it. Here, you seem to be arguing that because such effects are possible through extraordinary levels of power with Obtenebration, even its most rudimentary feats involve casually flitting past the Shroud. That's a questionable deduction to say the least.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's really telling that in one post your complaint was that Hecata apparently get to keep their unique powers, </div> </div> </div>What's really telling is that you think I was ever complaining about Hecata keeping unique powers from older editions in the first place, or that the discussion of power prerequisites for Oblivion Ceremonies is some sudden pivot from my earlier position. That misses the mark so wildly that I have to wonder if you even <i>skimmed</i> that part of the post, let alone read it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, what about Oblivion powers that have prerequisites?<br /> <br /> If this was really the design intent, wouldn't Touch of Oblivion have Ashes to Ashes as a prerequisite? They're adaptations of Grave's Decay and/or Mortis powers, depending on edition(...) </div> </div> </div>The prerequisites for necromancy-based Ceremonies clearly serve the purpose of gating access to them such that they won't be available if one chooses archetypically 'Lasombra' powers and the converse is true for the Shalimite Ceremonies if one pursues archetypically 'Hecata' powers. Compare this setup to Blood Sorcery, where there's no comparable effort to separate Tremere vs. Banu Haqim powersets since Rituals are freely available regardless of power selection. The difference obviously isn't coincidental, and given how questionably the prerequisite powers fit many of the necromancy-based Ceremonies, it would be absurd to assume that they simply went this route because it made so much sense at the time; the sensible conclusion is that despite merging Necromancy and Obtenebration as a singular Discipline in this edition, they still wanted to enforce some distance between them.<br /> <br /> As for the question you raise about including Oblivion powers in this arrangement, it really amounts to a question of why didn't they do more to corral the sets beyond Ceremonies. The answer is simple: Ceremony tracks are enough of a disincentive on their own. Focus on necromancy dictates power choice via necromantic Ceremony prerequisites, while someone who wants to focus on shadow-bending is naturally going to have to significantly sacrifice access to said Ceremonies in the process. Leaning even harder into this setup only increases the strain of the current setup in relation to other V5 Discipline design goals for little to no further gain.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The irony of this? Shadow Servant, a <i>level 3</i> Oblivion power, is an adaptation of a <i>level 1</i> Abyss Mysticism ritual. It also happens to be an <i>Auspex</i> amalgam, which by your own logic implies propriety to Hecata (because it's &quot;easier to get as Auspex is innate to Hecata&quot; or whatever). Right here's the example you're grasping at straws to find, yet you haven't mentioned it once. Why might that be? </div> </div> </div>This isn't really a distinct point in the argument I already addressed earlier, so it barely merits a separate response. But I will but since you think this is such a 'gotcha' moment:<br /> <br /> First, we've already established in the previous thread that I didn't buy the Player's Guide, so I'm not sure why you'd assume that I knew about the presence of this power in V5 at all, let alone the details of its prerequisites in particular. It wasn't even on my radar until you mentioned it and I went through the Paradox Wiki to check it out.<br /> <br /> Second, we're talking about an amalgam requirement of Auspex \u2022, which can cost exactly nothing regardless of clan depending on one's choice in predator type. I wouldn't call that 'harder' for anyone else to access in any meaningful way, and this really shouldn't be a surprise given my indifference to your earlier inclusion of Presence \u2022 (Daunt) for purposes of replicating older edition versions of Dementation, even if I didn't find it necessary either in that regard.<br /> <br /> Third, what exactly is this power in particular supposed to prove or disprove about of any my arguments that I'd specifically want to seek it out or avoid it in this thread?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","name":"Paramecius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27894_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-16T13:37:52-08:00","id":1528673,"title":"","content":"Myself I didn't care for the melding of the Obteneration with necromancer powers of the various death clans/bloodlines as they seem to take away from the uniqueness of both groups.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-18T17:42:31-08:00","id":1528857,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Paramecius</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1528630#post1528630\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Not really. I mentioned the Underworld...</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, no. You said the &quot;Underworld&quot;, you maybe even thought the &quot;Underworld&quot;, but you completely and accurately described the Labyrinth, not Oblivion. No points here for &quot;close enough&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The conceptual distance only grows if Oblivion itself is outright supposed to be Abyss. Firstly and most fundamentally, this is because Oblivion is entropy and destruction: the only sense in which it encompasses darkness is as the death of light. That's plenty for the literal definition of darkness, but that isn't what Obtenebration trades in: darkness as an elemental force unto itself that largely derives its destructive potential from its growing physical tangibility, as evidenced by Arms of the Abyss and Black Metamorphosis. </div> </div> </div>So why don't we just check that for veracity.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If Obtenebration derives from Oblivion, a more coherent progression of its capabilities be along the lines of actualizing ambient entropic potential: making flickering lights gutter out... </div> </div> </div>Which is what Obtenebration does. In older editions even Shadow Play did this, by reaching out to snuff out candles and small flashlights in close proximity to the vampire. Then, that rider got buffed and split off into Smothering Darkness, and the more powerful anti-light effect left to Shroud of Night and higher. If it were just a purely &quot;physical&quot; effect, it wouldn't affect electric lighting but alas it does, to the point high-powered directional lights are necessary to disable Obtenebration.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...exacerbating wounds and diseases rather than battering and choking with shadow tentacles... </div> </div> </div>Which is what Obtenebration does. Exactly what do you think the <i>Stamina</i> penalty for being in contact with Obtenebration darkness abstracts? If it were a purely &quot;physical&quot; effect, it wouldn't effect supernaturals that don't have to breathe, like for example <i>vampires</i>, and it certainly wouldn't effect supernaturals that don't even have physical bodies, like for example manifested wraiths and spectres.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...destabilizing substance rather than merging with darkness... </div> </div> </div>We'll get there, just not yet. I'm waiting because it'll be funnier when we do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...whereas the only point at which Obtenebration can directly unmake things in V20 is with near-Antedeluvian levels of power (Ahriman's Demesne). </div> </div> </div>Penny in the air...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If Oblivion is in fact part of the Underworld as the wiki states, the Shroud applies to it as well, barring extraordinary effects (such as elder-level Discipline powers) that can breach or bypass it. </div> </div> </div>Putting a in in this for now, penny's about to drop...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Here, you seem to be arguing that because such effects are possible through extraordinary levels of power with Obtenebration, even its most rudimentary feats involve casually flitting past the Shroud. That's a questionable deduction to say the least. </div> </div> </div>So, Maw of Ahriman is level 4 (3 in V5) and quite literally does exactly this. <i>All</i> of what you just said. Annihilates objects that come into contact with it, by opening a portal directly to Oblivion inside the vampire's mouth. Which quite neatly satisfies &quot;destabilizing substance&quot;, &quot;breaching or bypassing the Shroud&quot;, and at a level that isn't even exclusive to those below 8th generation.<br /> <br /> Neat, huh?<br /> <br /> So anyhow, that whole &quot;Shroud&quot; thing. Thinking about it more, I'd note also that in the MtAs/VtM crossover books to counter Obtenebration, a mage needs Spirit and Entropy. Strange combination that, for a power that by your logic should need only Forces to counter. It makes one wonder what else in the World of Darkness a mage needs specifically Spirit and Entropy to interact with...<br /> <br /> But I got thinking even more about Mage, and Obtenebration isn't even the only power that allows a vampire passage into the Umbra without interaction with the Shroud, Stormwall, or more pointedly, the Gauntlet in the bigger picture. Astral Projection, after all, drops the mind of the vampire using it straight into the Astral Umbra.<br /> <br /> Of course vampires don't call it such, because they have no real basis for understanding the Astral Umbra as mages do. Still the same place, right down to the silver cord, using mental attributes in lieu of physical, treating Willpower as health levels, and subjective distance/motion.<br /> <br /> Never mind for now the less-than-a-handful of blood sorcery paths and rituals which allow for interaction with the Penumbra.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The prerequisites for necromancy-based Ceremonies clearly serve the purpose of gating access to them such that they won't be available if one chooses archetypically 'Lasombra' powers and the converse is true for the Shalimite Ceremonies if one pursues archetypically 'Hecata' powers. </div> </div> </div>You're still woefully missing the mark. Oblivion is a la carte purchase because <i>there are no &quot;tracks&quot;</i>. A vampire can pick up Oblivion Sight at level 1 and any given available Oblivion power thereafter, because none of them have prerequisites. Which is, after all, a pretty important buy as without it vampires who would interact with wraiths have no way of perceiving them without Sense the Unseen -- like over half the Hecata bloodlines, and Lasombra.<br /> <br /> The idea &quot;this power is for Lasombra&quot; and &quot;this power is for Hecata&quot; simply no longer exists. Period.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Compare this setup to Blood Sorcery, where there's no comparable effort to separate Tremere vs. Banu Haqim powersets since Rituals are freely available regardless of power selection. </div> </div> </div>Yeah? The Banu Haqim who joined the Camarilla were schismatics, that is to say those who rebelled against ur-Shulgi when it awoke. Who were predominantly (but not exclusively) <i>Sorcerer-caste</i>.<br /> <br /> No kidding they can use blood sorcery, they <i>were</i> the clan's blood sorcerers.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The difference obviously isn't coincidental, and given how questionably the prerequisite powers fit many of the necromancy-based Ceremonies, it would be absurd to assume that they simply went this route because it made so much sense at the time; the sensible conclusion is that despite merging Necromancy and Obtenebration as a singular Discipline in this edition, they still wanted to enforce some distance between them. </div> </div> </div>Or, it was a desire to preserve game balanced, mixed with preventing players from inadvertently buying rituals or ceremonies they could never use. Summon Spirit has exactly frig-all use if the vampire with it can't identify a fetter, and none of the major Oblivion ceremonies to do with wraiths and the Shadowlands have real viability if the vampire can't identify the Shroud's thickness where they're attempting the ritual.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This isn't really a distinct point in the argument I already addressed earlier, so it barely merits a separate response. But I will but since you think this is such a 'gotcha' moment: </div> </div> </div>Well, you certainly seemed to feel otherwise when it came to &quot;innate&quot; access to Chimerstry for Setites. Glad to see you've come around!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> First, we've already established in the previous thread that I didn't buy the Player's Guide, so I'm not sure why you'd assume that I knew about the presence of this power in V5 at all, let alone the details of its prerequisites in particular. It wasn't even on my radar until you mentioned it and I went through the Paradox Wiki to check it out. </div> </div> </div>Now we get to the rub of it. You're barely willing to engage in reading on the topic you claim to be defending, can't present the information you claim to be employing accurately, and certainly won't do your due diligence on the material you're criticizing to have an informed opinion of it. Even when that information is freely available to anyone with internet access which you clearly have to be posting here, and not within the confines of a paid resource like a downloaded PDF or a physical book.<br /> <br /> Instead, you're posting r/IAmVerySmart walls of text and playing semantics, hoping no one will catch whiff of the manure you're shoveling to cover for the fact you're just looking for reasons to indulge your preconceived distaste for V5. You're not out to engage in good-faith fact-finding on this, you're looking to have your biases against V5 confirmed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-19T18:22:40-08:00","id":1528971,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1528857#post1528857\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which is what Obtenebration does. In older editions even Shadow Play did this, by reaching out to snuff out candles and small flashlights in close proximity to the vampire. Then, that rider got buffed and split off into Smothering Darkness, and the more powerful anti-light effect left to Shroud of Night and higher. If it were just a purely &quot;physical&quot; effect, it wouldn't affect electric lighting but alas it does, to the point high-powered directional lights are necessary to disable Obtenebration.</div> </div> </div> </div>Darkness from Obtenebration is first conjured or animated and then snuffs out light by coming into contact with it. The more 'natural' use of Oblivion to darken areas would be to sap heat, render fuel inert, or disrupt electricity or the means by which it is conveyed to render the continued production of light impossible, leaving only darkness behind. The basic applications of Obtenebration reverse this process: Shroud of Night explicitly creates darkness and this darkness then needs to engulf light sources to affect them. Smothering Darkness functions in the same way.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Which is what Obtenebration does. Exactly what do you think the <i>Stamina</i> penalty for being in contact with Obtenebration darkness abstracts? If it were a purely &quot;physical&quot; effect, it wouldn't effect supernaturals that don't have to breathe, like for example <i>vampires</i>, and it certainly wouldn't effect supernaturals that don't even have physical bodies, like for example manifested wraiths and spectres. </div> </div> </div>Let's work with an actual quote from Shadow Play: &quot;Once a Kindred takes control of darkness or shadow, it gains a mystical tangibility. By varying accounts cold or hellishly hot and cloying, the darkness may be used to aggravate or even smother victims.&quot; No mention of preexisting injuries, diseases, or whatever.<br /> <br /> The harrowing nature of contact with the darkness from Shadow Play is obviously part of the cause for the penalty that others take from it (or even all of the cause for things that don't breathe), but feeling unpleasant, or even horrific, isn't synonymous with exacerbating existing weaknesses. We're talking about an external force inducing a new stimulus through contact, whereas the easiest and simplest application of Oblivion's influence, and thus the one most appropriate for a one-dot power for a Discipline centered around this, would be to augment frailties and afflictions that are already present; clearly, that's not how this power works.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, Maw of Ahriman is level 4 (3 in V5) and quite literally does exactly this. <i>All</i> of what you just said. Annihilates objects that come into contact with it, by opening a portal directly to Oblivion inside the vampire's mouth. Which quite neatly satisfies &quot;destabilizing substance&quot;, &quot;breaching or bypassing the Shroud&quot;, and at a level that isn't even exclusive to those below 8th generation.<br /> <br /> Neat, huh? </div> </div> </div>It's a fun ritual, but as you've noted, Maw of Ahriman is from Abyss Mysticism. As you're no doubt aware, Abyss Mysticism requires dots in both Obtenebration and Occult to use. The powers of Obtenebration are comparatively intuitive and 'natural' to users, whereas Abyss Mysticism Rituals take more work to master. The Ritual we're talking about here should be one of the most straightforward ways to harness the forces of Oblivion, even if raw power would be needed to do so to this extent, but somehow the process of materializing nothingness via Arms of Ahriman to tentacle slap people is less convoluted and easier to grasp; again, the logical order for a Discipline supposedly centered around tapping into the essence of Oblivion is reversed.<br /> <br /> As for breaching or bypassing the Shroud, as you've noted, the ritual comes into play at 4th level in previous editions, near the utmost limit of what vampires of the 8th Generation and above can achieve, and with the notable constraint that the rift it produces is limited to the <i>inside of the ritualist's mouth</i>. Even without being an elder power, it's a weak basis for assuming the baseline ways in which all Obtenebration powers function from start to finish, especially when even the earliest powers would have to entail creating larger breaches in the Shroud from a longer distance away if that was how they truly function and the very first power would have to achieve this feat without even requiring a roll, something which Maw of Ahriman itself can't do.<br /> <br /> Still, it's true that Maw of Ahriman is a damn sight more accessible than Ahriman's Demesne. It's technically not from Obtenebration itself and is subject to the general fact that Abyss Mysticism is the former's esoteric cousin covering the things that take more work and specialized knowledge to accomplish, but it's definitely more accessible.<br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So anyhow, that whole &quot;Shroud&quot; thing. Thinking about it more, I'd note also that in the MtAs/VtM crossover books to counter Obtenebration, a mage needs Spirit and Entropy. (...) It makes one wonder what else in the World of Darkness a mage needs specifically Spirit and Entropy to interact with... </div> </div> </div>Mage isn't my game any more than Wraith is, but even I know that the Spheres aren't simple or static enough that you can make specific conclusions as to the singular, definitive nature of a thing merely on the basis of the Spheres used to affect it; a requirement of Spirit + Entropy might indicate a wraith, for example, or another entity that exists in the spaces where these Spheres intersect, like a spirit of good (or ill) fortune, or a bane that embodies decay. I've also heard that the flexibility of Spheres is such that feats typically associated with one can be functionally replicated through others (though perhaps in combination with other Spheres or with greater difficulty), and that individual paradigms can affect the sorts of Spheres required to use magic and how said Spheres can be used; both of these things seem like they would muddy the waters further.<br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Strange combination that, for a power that by your logic should need only Forces to counter. </div> </div> </div>Not by my logic unless you take my use of the words &quot;elemental darkness&quot; to mean that I have disavowed any otherworldly origin to Obtenebration to insist that the darkness it creates/influences is a base element of the physical world instead of a primordial essence of darkness, which of course you would.<br /> <br /> Or maybe you're basing this assessment on what I said about Obtenebration's destructive potential largely coming into play as it becomes possible to make it more physically tangible: this is demonstrated by the fact that you can't directly deal damage to others with Obtenebration prior to Arms of Ahriman, but that's not the same as saying that the darkness from the Discipline is purely physical and lacking any spiritual component. Not even when using Arms of Ahriman itself: spirits are still spirits (and spirit parts are still spirit parts) when they corporealize in the living world, after all.\u200b<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Astral Projection, after all, drops the mind of the vampire using it straight into the Astral Umbra.<br /> (...)<br /> Never mind for now the less-than-a-handful of blood sorcery paths and rituals which allow for interaction with the Penumbra. </div> </div> </div>Sure, and Astral Projection's a five-dot power. You're still failing to make a case for the most basic of basic Obtenebration powers pulling off the same thing, much less without requiring a roll.<br /> <br /> And that's a big sticking point where looking to other Paths and Rituals for precedent is concerned: only blood is required to activate Shadow Play, at which point the animation of surrounding darkness is a matter of course barring intervention from other forces. By default, the only roll required is Courage for mortals to avoid being creeped out to the point of taking a penalty.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A vampire can pick up Oblivion Sight at level 1 and any given available Oblivion power thereafter, because none of them have prerequisites. Which is, after all, a pretty important buy as without it vampires who would interact with wraiths have no way of perceiving them without Sense the Unseen -- like over half the Hecata bloodlines, and Lasombra. </div> </div> </div>Not having Auspex as a clan Discipline doesn't make Sense the Unseen a particularly elusive replacement for Oblivion Sight, given that anyone can get it for free through a predator type.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The idea &quot;this power is for Lasombra&quot; and &quot;this power is for Hecata&quot; simply no longer exists. Period. </div> </div> </div>Not really. It's not as firmly enforced as in earlier editions, but clan descriptions, themes, and amalgam requirements that aren't trivially met still serve to support associations. These associations are strengthened further by the presence of Rituals and Ceremonies, given that unlike Discipline powers they always require teachers. Unless you're suggesting that the Lasombra retroactively have a significant and established tradition of necromancers to tap into for Ceremonies on this side of things, then access to them still largely falls within Hecata territory given the general need for Hecata teachers barring rare exceptions. I'd say the same goes for Ceremonies relating to the shadow-bending side of things, but even though it's probably still true that it's mostly Lasombra who learn and thus can teach these Ceremonies, I call them Shalimite Ceremonies for a reason: the default assumption is that the Cult of Shalim is loath to share them with outsiders.<br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yeah? The Banu Haqim who joined the Camarilla were schismatics, that is to say those who rebelled against ur-Shulgi when it awoke. Who were predominantly (but not exclusively) <i>Sorcerer-caste</i>.<br /> <br /> No kidding they can use blood sorcery, they <i>were</i> the clan's blood sorcerers. </div> </div> </div>The point isn't that it's surprising that they can use Blood Sorcery. The point is that there is no gating of specific Blood Sorcery Rituals regardless of the powers chosen, despite both clans historically having distinctive Rituals of their own; I'd think this would be hard to miss given that I mentioned Rituals in the same sentence, but the fact that you did anyway is par for the course at this point.<br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or, it was a desire to preserve game balanced, mixed with preventing players from inadvertently buying rituals or ceremonies they could never use. Summon Spirit has exactly frig-all use if the vampire with it can't identify a fetter, and none of the major Oblivion ceremonies to do with wraiths and the Shadowlands have real viability if the vampire can't identify the Shroud's thickness where they're attempting the ritual. </div> </div> </div>You're missing a few things here:<br /> 1. Ceremonies don't have to be used alone even if you're the one making the roll, meaning that others can assist in various ways - including by using other powers.<br /> 2. Powers from other Disciplines can potentially serve as functional substitutes for certain Oblivion powers, like Unseen Sense for Oblivion Sight and Spirit's Touch for Binding Fetter (though likely with a bit of Investigation and Occult as well). Unseen Sense, Investigation, and Occult can also aid in deducing the density of the Shroud in an area, but besides that, you don't really need to know the density if you can weaken the Shroud regardless, such as via Split the Shroud or more gradually by other means: Occult can reveal that death, malaise, and use of necromancy can lower the Shroud's density, for example, and it's easy to make sure these things aren't in short supply in many areas.<br /> 3. Currently only one Ceremony that utilizes a fetter has The Binding Fetter as a prerequisite, and no Ceremonies that summon spirits require Oblivion Sight. So by your reasoning here, they've failed in their goal to protect players from trap options they supposedly can't use.<br /> 4. Prerequisites for many other necromantic Ceremonies that serve neither as a practical nor thematic foundation of the Ceremonies themselves, listed from lowest to highest level:<ul><li><b>The Gift of False Life:</b> Reanimates a mindless corpse to do chores, requiring Ashes to Ashes, which does basically the opposite, for some reason.</li> <li><b>Ashen Relic</b>: Preserves the body of a slain Kindred, requiring Ashes to Ashes, which again does the opposite, or Oblivion Sight, which has nothing to do with the Ceremony whatsoever, for some reason.</li> <li><b>Awaken the Homuncular Servant:</b> Reanimates severed body parts or small animal carcasses with your Vitae, requiring Where the Shroud Thins for some reason.\u200b</li> <li><b>Host Spirit:</b> Binds a wraith within your body, requiring Aura of Decay, the power to make flesh and base matter rot, for some reason.</li> <li><b>Wisdom of the Dead</b>: Question a severed head or skull. Requires Ashes to Ashes or Oblivion Sight; the latter's not terrible as a choice, but it's a significantly weaker thematic fit than Fatal Precognition.</li> <li><b>Bind the Spirit:</b> Guess. Requires Necrotic Plague, the power to spread diseases to mortals, for some reason.</li> <li><b>Split the Shroud:</b> Guess. Also requires Necrotic Plague for some reason.</li> <li><b>Ex Nihilo:</b> Allows you to step bodily into the Shadowlands. Requires Withering Spirit, which drains victims of Willpower potentially until their souls shrivel to nothing, for some reason; perhaps ironically, Tenebrous Avatar would've made more sense (but of course they couldn't go with that because necromancy).</li> </ul>And an honorable mention for the Shalimite Ceremony <b>Traveler's Call</b>, which serves to contact other cultists and requires Oblivion Sight despite having nothing to do with seeing wraiths or even seeing in darkness.<br /> <br /> Other Ceremonies have prerequisites that seem fairly appropriate to me, or seemingly no prerequisites at all for The Knowing Stone and Maw of Ahriman according to the Wiki. Still, that's a hefty chunk and it started out worse.<br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, you certainly seemed to feel otherwise when it came to &quot;innate&quot; access to Chimerstry for Setites. Glad to see you've come around! </div> </div> </div>I'm genuinely curious to learn what passes for the reasoning behind this nonsense.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now we get to the rub of it. You're barely willing to engage in reading on the topic you claim to be defending, can't present the information you claim to be employing accurately, and certainly won't do your due diligence on the material you're criticizing to have an informed opinion of it. Even when that information is freely available to anyone with internet access which you clearly have to be posting here, and not within the confines of a paid resource like a downloaded PDF or a physical book. </div> </div> </div>A brief timeline of events in our particular discussion of this power:<br /> 1. You mention the power in the previous post and ask me why I didn't bring it up myself, seemingly implying dishonest or evasive motives on my part.<br /> 2. I ask why you'd even assume I was already familiar with the V5 version of this same power when I don't own the book before going on to address it based on the description from the Paradox Wiki.<br /> 3. You quote me and try to prop that quote up as proof of my laziness for not having bothered to look up the power online, <i>while ignoring part of the same quoted paragraph where I mentioned going to the Paradox Wiki to look it up</i> and, of course, the next paragraph where I mention why its one-dot Auspex requirement is irrelevant (hint: it involves predator types).<br /> <br /> This would be an almost unbelievably incompetent example of bad-faith argumentation, so I'm more inclined to consider this yet another sign that you're in such a rush to bark at my posts that you can't be bothered to make sure of what I'm even saying. Then again, both possibilities probably amount to more or less the same thing in practice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","name":"Paramecius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27894_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-20T10:24:23-08:00","id":1529016,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Paramecius</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate#post1528172\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> or more specifically about Serpentis in previous editions vs their use of Protean in V5. I</div> </div> </div> </div>I definitely prefer Followers of Set ( or Ministers ) with Serpentis instead of Protean. This Discipline does come across as clearly appropriate for Setites, when I consider how that the methods, ideaology, and also of course Clan culture of these Vampires is portrayed and described as specialized, gripping, and intense.<br /> The characterfulness and themes of Serpentis are focused and interesting. Certainly from reading about the Powers of this Discipline some good and worthy thoughts and ideas can drawn and developed.<br /> Also this Discipline seems to be rather exclusive to Setites, which is a welcome detail and addition to the VtM as a setting and a game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-22T13:21:32-08:00","id":1529170,"title":"","content":"As a side I'm not really sure why the ministry has protean, its not really thematically much to do with how they work. Serpantis kind of made sense for setites but the ministry would probably make more sense with blood sorcery or auspez to reflect their cult mo.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-22T23:42:32-08:00","id":1529209,"title":"","content":"Blood sorcery indeed would make more sense, but Akhu was unique, V5 can't really handle it<br /> But with some mental gymnastics, Protean could make sense for a Clan from Egypt and the releted relegion<br /> <br /> <b>Eyes of the Beast: </b>The vampire can will a supernatural red gleam into their eyes, giving them sight even in the total absence of light.<br /> - this could be reference to the red eyes of Sekhmet, who almost made humanity extinct, or Ra's other vengefull Eye (eyes, there were many), or the boody Eye of Horus Set tore out<br /> <br /> <b>Feral Weapons:</b> The vampire can extend their natural weapons to monstrous proportions. This usually takes the form of fingernails extending into wicked talons but can also come in other forms such as fangs elongating into veritable daggers, as from a gigantic serpent.<br /> - Again, Set was a warrior god, and Egypt was full of predatory gods with featrues a vampire could &quot;borrow&quot;<br /> <br /> <b>Earth Meld</b>: Becoming one with the soil, the vampire sinks into the earth. Unless in torpor, the vampire rises again the following night. - Geb, the God of Earth was Set's father...<br /> or <b>Shapechange</b>: The vampire can assume the shape of an animal roughly the same size as their original mass.<br /> - this is easy, the so called &quot;the <b>Set animal</b>, or <i><b>sha</b></i>,\u200b&quot; (a slender canid, resembling a greyhound or a jackal, with three distinguishing features: a stiff tail, often forked at the end, which stands straight up or at an angle, whether the animal is sitting, standing, or walking; its ears, also held erect, are usually depicted as squarish or triangular, narrowest at the base and widest at the squarish tops; and a long nose, often with a slight downward curve. It is normally depicted as black, but may also have been reddish) but some Egyptian texts suggest that Set took the form of a dangerous animal, such as a bull or crocodile<br /> <br /> <b>Metamorphosis:</b> This power grants an additional animal form to the user, this time also enabling them to change their size.<br /> - upgrade the Set animal to the &quot;Typhonic Beast&quot;, a larger Set Animal<br /> <br /> <b>Mist Form</b>: The vampire gains the legendary power to turn into a cloud of mist, perceivable to the eye but untouchable by anything save fire, sunlight, and supernatural aggression. They can fit through pipes, crevices, and cracks. While strong winds might buffet them, no natural force can disperse the cloud.<br /> - Became the dark and cold mist of Duat<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-23T13:41:29-08:00","id":1529243,"title":"","content":"I don't have a problem with Followers of Set/Ministry having Protean at all. In the early days of VTR I tried to make a Mekhet bloodline that was inspired by FOS and gave them that discipline in addition to their in clan disciplines.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-23T23:24:22-08:00","id":1529288,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1529209#post1529209\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Blood sorcery indeed would make more sense, but Akhu was unique, V5 can't really handle it<br /> But with some mental gymnastics, Protean could make sense for a Clan from Egypt and the releted relegion<br /> <br /> <b>Eyes of the Beast: </b>The vampire can will a supernatural red gleam into their eyes, giving them sight even in the total absence of light.<br /> - this could be reference to the red eyes of Sekhmet, who almost made humanity extinct, or Ra's other vengefull Eye (eyes, there were many), or the boody Eye of Horus Set tore out<br /> <br /> <b>Feral Weapons:</b> The vampire can extend their natural weapons to monstrous proportions. This usually takes the form of fingernails extending into wicked talons but can also come in other forms such as fangs elongating into veritable daggers, as from a gigantic serpent.<br /> - Again, Set was a warrior god, and Egypt was full of predatory gods with featrues a vampire could &quot;borrow&quot;<br /> <br /> <b>Earth Meld</b>: Becoming one with the soil, the vampire sinks into the earth. Unless in torpor, the vampire rises again the following night. - Geb, the God of Earth was Set's father...<br /> or <b>Shapechange</b>: The vampire can assume the shape of an animal roughly the same size as their original mass.<br /> - this is easy, the so called &quot;the <b>Set animal</b>, or <i><b>sha</b></i>,&quot; (a slender canid, resembling a greyhound or a jackal, with three distinguishing features: a stiff tail, often forked at the end, which stands straight up or at an angle, whether the animal is sitting, standing, or walking; its ears, also held erect, are usually depicted as squarish or triangular, narrowest at the base and widest at the squarish tops; and a long nose, often with a slight downward curve. It is normally depicted as black, but may also have been reddish) but some Egyptian texts suggest that Set took the form of a dangerous animal, such as a bull or crocodile<br /> <br /> <b>Metamorphosis:</b> This power grants an additional animal form to the user, this time also enabling them to change their size.<br /> - upgrade the Set animal to the &quot;Typhonic Beast&quot;, a larger Set Animal<br /> <br /> <b>Mist Form</b>: The vampire gains the legendary power to turn into a cloud of mist, perceivable to the eye but untouchable by anything save fire, sunlight, and supernatural aggression. They can fit through pipes, crevices, and cracks. While strong winds might buffet them, no natural force can disperse the cloud.<br /> - Became the dark and cold mist of Duat<br /> <br /> \u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not a bad go but at that stage you're doing do much leg work any discipline counts eh potence as set was a fierce warrior who fought apothis or Dominate due to aspirations of dominion. Plus you're re wedging back into thr Set mould which 5th ed tries to downplay.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-24T02:44:51-08:00","id":1529294,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1529288#post1529288\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">you're re wedging back into thr Set mould which 5th ed tries to downplay.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is actually a common misconception<br /> The Ministry's full name is Ministry of Set - tho' the Followers of Set of Africa, the Walid Set (Family of Set- mostly in the lands of Arabia and North Africa, the ones on the Ashirra territory) or Followers of Egyptian descent still uses the Followers of Set name - and all of them, regardless of the name technically could refer themeselves as the <b>Mesu Bedshet</b>, the &quot;Children of Rebellion&quot;.<br /> Sure &quot;Orthodox Setites&quot; exist, like the Church of Set, but calling themselves the Ministry of Set was just a <i>rebranding</i> to update themselves for the times and to reflect the clan's understanding of its relationship to its founder had changed \u2013 rather than being Set's followers, they see Set as being <i>within them</i>, making them his living church.<br /> So the connection to Set remained - and to them, like in previous editions - Set was/is behind in many names, like Lord Shiva, Typhon, Loki and so on, basically to infiltrate and use any given relegion in the area<br /> (Actually the rebranding &quot;Ministiry&quot; or Ministiry of Set is the biggest joke they could have pulled of - &quot;Ministry&quot; is from the Greek word diakoneo, meaning &quot;to serve&quot; or douleuo, meaning &quot;to serve as a slave.&quot; In the New Testament, ministry is seen as service to God and to other people in His name. The term &quot;ministry&quot; implies a sense of being duty-bound to work under God's authority as His loyal servant. And in their case this God is Set, who is in all of them)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","user":"Shadeprowler","date":". 01-24-2024, 06:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-29T06:07:28-08:00","id":1529641,"title":"","content":"Some kind of blood magic makes more sense for the Ministry than Protean, really. But the problem there is how much Blood Sorcery has become overly focused on blood in V5.<br /> <br /> Obtenebration would probably also have made more sense as a general blood sorcery path of shadows, if Blood Sorcery hadn't become so narrow. That way, Abyss Mysticism would just be another tradition of blood magic, like Akhu or Dur-An-Ki. You wouldn't need to force any parallels between the Abyss and the Underworld, unless you said all blood magic came from the same source (which you shouldn't).<br /> <br /> If you limit a character's blood magic paths and rituals to their tradition's themes, you could even fold in Necromancy too. The difference would be that a necromancer would have to take paths that relate to their themes of death, decay and the Underworld, but there might be a few &quot;crossover&quot; paths that could be taken by multiple traditions (e.g., Hands of Destruction could be flavoured as fire for elementalists and kolduns/entropy or ageing for necromancers/wrath of the gods for ashippu/corrosive shadows for Abyss Mystics, etc).<br /> <br /> This would work especially well for things like Akhu and Wanga, which always sort of crossed the boundaries between Thaumaturgy and Necromancy, anyway. Suddenly, they wouldn't need Thaumaturgy paths that replicated Necromancy powers, because those traditions would just get access to the same paths (filtered through the appropriate cultural lens).<br /> <br /> You'd cut down a lot of the redundancy, you wouldn't need to force connections between things that don't need them, and each strand of blood magic could be different again. You can also build limitations into magic as a default, because each blood magician can only learn magic that fits into their worldview.<br /> <br /> Banu Haqim might get a list of paths that favour combat, demon-hunting and judgment, while the Lasombra might get a list that focuses on primordial darkness, corruption and extradimensional spaces (e.g., putting people in shadow prisons or travelling through shadows). If you want a weird or unique path, you have to justify how it fits your &quot;paradigm&quot;, and you may need to adjust it with your Storyteller to make it a better fit.<br /> <br /> It works better to stop blood sorcerers being good at everything, too.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200bGetting back to the core topic, though: for me, VTR2e's approach is best. Streamlined but broad core Disciplines + Devotions seems the best way to handle it. The pick and mix approach increases decision paralysis and creates trap builds, by its very nature.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-29T12:31:23-08:00","id":1529659,"title":"","content":"I'm not a big fan of Blood Sorcery in general I'd rather have the FOS/Ministry have /Protean/Serpentis myself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-30T12:16:30-08:00","id":1529729,"title":"","content":"I wonder if anyone worked out a revised version of the Baali Discipline of Damioan for the current setting?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-30T13:25:43-08:00","id":1529734,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lysander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1529729#post1529729\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wonder if anyone worked out a revised version of the Baali Discipline of Damioan for the current setting?</div> </div> </div> </div>Before I gave up on v5 I was playing with it being another type of Oblivion powers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-31T18:38:53-08:00","id":1529793,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lysander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1529729#post1529729\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wonder if anyone worked out a revised version of the Baali Discipline of Damioan for the current setting?</div> </div> </div> </div>For what it's worth, when I homebrewed that Baali loresheet, I took a good, critical, look at the discipline to see exactly what it did versus other, comparable disciplines and powers. Honestly like I said over there, Daimonion is basically just a grab bag...a well-designed grab bag, but still a grab bag. The only power truly unique is Psychomachia and that's kind of a stretch given its similarity to older-edition Fata Morgana, as even Condemnation is a catch-all for various blood sorcery powers like those of Evil Eye, Curses, Prey on the Soul's Fear, and a few I'm sure I've forgotten.<br /> <br /> Where I landed after that, is everything Daimonion did can be represented (in the remit of v5 mechanics) just fine by disciplines and powers that already exist. The lone exception being Sense the Sin, which is why I made it a unique variant to the Premonition power. Other than that, give characters something to empower that which they already get versus vampires, and ensure they have access to Oblivion and Blood Sorcery via merit, and it's pretty much done.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-03T01:43:05-08:00","id":1529888,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1529734#post1529734\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Before I gave up on v5 I was playing with it being another type of Oblivion powers.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this is the best way to do it. The STV versions of the Baali I've seen all had Daimonion as Oblivion. It's also perfect for the Angellis Ater/Azaneali.<br /> <br /> They could probably pick up Blood Sorcery via a Loresheet or Predator Type of they wanted to go all-in on the occult thing. The Order of Moloch might take Blood Sorcery instead of Oblivion, to represent their anti-infernalist thing and links with the Banu Haqim/Dur-An-Ki.<br /> <br /> Apostates could also just be handled as a Loresheet.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-03T11:26:05-08:00","id":1529921,"title":"","content":"Oblivion could work with the Baali. If we ever use them in our game at all and I'm thinking to tell our GM to make them a cult and not a bloodline at all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-03T16:08:06-08:00","id":1529942,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>adambeyoncelowe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1529888#post1529888\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think this is the best way to do it. The STV versions of the Baali I've seen all had Daimonion as Oblivion. It's also perfect for the Angellis Ater/Azaneali.<br /> <br /> They could probably pick up Blood Sorcery via a Loresheet or Predator Type of they wanted to go all-in on the occult thing. The Order of Moloch might take Blood Sorcery instead of Oblivion, to represent their anti-infernalist thing and links with the Banu Haqim/Dur-An-Ki.<br /> <br /> Apostates could also just be handled as a Loresheet.</div> </div> </div> </div>Theirs something to that, I tended to play with giving the baali dark thaumaturgy as their clan discipline in revised/v20 for v5 give them the standard lineup and slap a load of nasty rituals on doing some stuff really out of left field.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-04T08:50:25-08:00","id":1529969,"title":"","content":"I had considered letting them steal Blood Sorcery rituals and converting them to Oblivion ceremonies (they'd still gain stains for using them, in addition to the normal cost), with a Loresheet, but that might be OP. It would be very flavoursome, though.<br /> <br /> The catch would be that these stolen rituals would always manifest in a thematically suitable way -- there's always a taint of death, decay, corruption, etc -- because they're twisted versions of normal magic. It lends itself to Baali who need to infiltrate other organisations to steal occult lore, which is very fitting, IMO.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":5291,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","user":"adambeyoncelowe","date":". 02-04-2024, 11:57 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-06-08T01:27:48-07:00","id":1536953,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MrDrika</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate?p=1528622#post1528622\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now [Lasombra] is a conscious part of the OBLIVION and facing reaction of their denizens? And the Amici Noctis go to Oblivion to do planning and controlling the clan? I mean, wouldn't be more sensible a Umbral realm instead of the (now very populated) Oblivion? And of course, if the Lasombras are manipulating the energies of Oblivion, not anything else, literally anything else, their power is lethal. Remembering that before being a name of a discipline, the Oblivion is the final, total and utterly destruction.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've always thought that Lasombra took over the consciousness of Gratiano, and is now living the life he always envied as a mortal: he's now handsome, rich, adored, and is still powerful enough to take on any Methuselah on the planet. Win - win.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25666-illuminostro","name":"Illuminostro","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"166"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-06-08T01:31:58-07:00","id":1536954,"title":"","content":"White Wolf: &quot;Well, you see, The Abyss is the place where demons were imprisoned, but not the same Abyss where some ghosts are, but not the same place where shadow shit we never cared to think out that the Lasombra can somehow summon, but can sometimes be eaten by, but somehow became fused with said demigod Antediluvian with 83 names that rhyme with La-Som-Bra. Hope that clears it up until we hire new writers to fuck it up further. Fuck off, nerds.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25666-illuminostro","name":"Illuminostro","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"166"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}